    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Tekton sockets are made in Taiwan to Tekton's own specification, sold mostly in 6-point, and backed by a no-receipt lifetime warranty. The useful questions are which line you need (chrome or impact), which drive sizes, and whether a big set is better value than building your own from rails. This review answers those from Tekton's published specs.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="the-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Tekton Socket Range at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton splits its sockets into two families. Hand-drive sockets are chrome-plated and come in 1/4-, 3/8- and 1/2-inch drive, in 6-point and 12-point, and in shallow, semi-deep and deep lengths. Impact sockets have a dark phosphate finish and come in 1/4-, 3/8-, 1/2- and 3/4-inch drive. Both families include SAE and metric sizes, along with specialty sockets such as bit, external star, universal joint, spark plug and oxygen sensor sockets on the hand side, and lug nut and axle nut sockets on the impact side.
          </p>

          <p>
            There used to be a 1-inch drive impact set, but Tekton's page for that 9-piece set (item 4892) now says it has been removed from the current lineup, and the 6-point impact category lists drive sizes only up to 3/4 inch. If you need 1-inch drive for heavy equipment, Tekton is no longer the place to look.
          </p>

          <p>
            What sets Tekton apart is how the range is organised rather than any single socket. As our <a href="/where-are-tekton-tools-made/">guide to where Tekton tools are made</a> describes, the company sells one line of tools arranged by size and type, builds sets from the same parts it sells individually, and avoids skipping sizes. The 10 mm socket you replace after losing one is the same part that came in your set. Most sets come on Tekton's twist-to-lock socket rails, which are made in the USA.
          </p>

          <p>
            That approach shapes how you should buy. Rather than picking one huge kit, you can buy a set in the drive size you use most and add rails of deep or metric sockets as gaps appear. Our <a href="/best-tekton-hand-tools/">best Tekton hand tools guide</a> puts the sockets in context with the wrenches and ratchets, and our <a href="/tekton-tools-review/">full Tekton tools review</a> covers the brand as a whole.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="chrome-sockets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Chrome Hand Sockets: Steel, Finish and Markings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton publishes more detail about its sockets than most brands at this price. Taking the 73-piece 3/8-inch drive set (SKT15311) as an example, the product page lists 50BV30 chromium-vanadium steel for the semi-deep and deep sockets and 6140 chromium-vanadium for the shallow sockets and extensions. The exterior is chrome-plated for corrosion resistance. Inside, Tekton says the sockets are chrome-plated and then given a clear coat or paint where needed, because plating does not always reach the bottom of a deep bore.
          </p>

          <p>
            The size markings on chrome sockets are roll-stamped into the metal rather than printed. Tekton says it makes them as large as the curved surface allows, with an extra-deep outline. That matters more than it sounds: printed or shallow-etched markings on cheap sockets wear off within a few years of rattling around a drawer, and a stamped number stays readable for as long as you own the socket.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 6-point openings use what Tekton calls radiused corners, which apply force to the flat sides of a fastener rather than its corners. This is the same idea that other brands market as flank drive, and it is the main reason a 6-point socket is less likely to round off a tight or corroded bolt. Small sizes have stepped, narrowed shoulders for reach into recesses. The drive end has four detent grooves, so the socket grips the ratchet's retaining ball whichever way you push it on.
          </p>

          <p>
            On strength, the 73-piece set's page says proof torque meets at least 1.2 times the ANSI standard in a single-cycle test, and some of Tekton's rail sets are listed at 1.3 times. Proof torque is the load a socket must withstand without permanent deformation, so either figure means a margin above the industry minimum rather than a headline number. We have not measured it ourselves; we are reporting Tekton's published figures.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Chrome sockets are for hand tools only.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Tekton's chrome sockets are built for ratchets and breaker bars. Run them on an impact wrench and the hard, brittle chrome-vanadium can crack. Buy the impact line for impact tools.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="impact-sockets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Impact Sockets: What Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton's impact sockets differ from the chrome line in finish, markings and wall thickness. Every piece has a corrosion-resistant manganese phosphate coating instead of chrome plating. Chrome can flake under repeated hammering, which is why impact sockets across the industry use a dark oxide or phosphate finish. Each impact socket carries its size twice: a laser marking on the front for quick reading and a stamped marking on the back that will outlast it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The steel story is more nuanced than the usual rule that chrome sockets are chrome-vanadium and impact sockets are chrome-molybdenum. Tekton's page for the 21-piece 1/2-inch drive impact set (SID92102) lists 50BV30 chromium-vanadium for the 5/16- to 1-1/4-inch sockets and 4140 chromium-molybdenum only for the larger 1-5/16- to 2-inch sizes. The 68-piece 3/8-inch impact set (SID91218) lists 50BV30 for all its sockets. Tekton's own alloy names sit outside the simple rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not make them chrome sockets in disguise. What suits a socket to impact use is the whole specification: heat treatment that keeps it tougher and less brittle, thicker walls, and a finish that will not chip. The alloy label is one input. Our <a href="/chrome-vanadium-vs-chrome-molybdenum-sockets/">chrome vanadium vs chrome molybdenum guide</a> explains the general difference. The practical rule stays the same: use sockets sold as impact sockets on impact tools, whatever steel the page lists.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two design details are worth noting. Tekton's shallow impact sockets have a full-length well inside, so a long bolt that pokes through a nut does not bottom out as quickly as it would in a standard shallow socket. And the sockets are designed to work with both friction-ring and detent-pin anvils, so they suit whichever style your impact wrench has. If you are still choosing a gun, our <a href="/best-impact-wrench/">impact wrench guide</a> covers the anvil question.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="socket-sets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Tekton Socket Set to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton sells dozens of sets, and the catalog runs from 8-piece rails to a 250-piece set in a rolling cart. Rather than ranking all of them, here are representative sets from Tekton's site with their verified contents. Prices are Tekton.com list prices at the time of writing and change often.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Set</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Drive</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Contents</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">List price</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">SKT15311, 73-piece with ratchet and case</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3/8 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/4-1 in and 6-24 mm, shallow and deep; 90-tooth ratchet, 18 in breaker bar, extensions, U-joint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$195</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">SHD90220, 87-piece on rails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/4 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5/32-11/16 in and 4-17 mm, rails and trays</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$195</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">SHD92207, 46-piece on rails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/2 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-32 mm, shallow and deep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$210</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">SID91218, 68-piece impact on rails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3/8 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/4-1 in and 6-24 mm, shallow and deep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$240</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">SID92102, 21-piece impact on rails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/2 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5/16-1-1/2 in shallow</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$130</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For most people starting out, a 3/8-inch drive set that includes both shallow and deep sockets in SAE and metric is the core purchase. The SKT15311 is a good example of what a complete one looks like: every size from 1/4 inch to 1 inch and 6 mm to 24 mm in both depths, plus a 90-tooth ratchet with a 4-degree swing arc, extensions, a universal joint and an 18-inch breaker bar. Our guide to <a href="/what-drive-size-socket-set-do-i-need/">which drive size you need</a> explains why 3/8 inch covers most car and home jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add 1/4-inch drive for small fasteners in tight spaces, and 1/2-inch drive for suspension, lug nuts and anything that needs a breaker bar. If you own an impact wrench, buy impact sockets for it rather than stretching the chrome set. The rail-mounted sets suit drawer storage. Cased sets suit a car trunk or a job site, though the blow-molded case is bulky.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be careful with the very large combination kits. A 212-piece set looks like better value per piece, but the count includes bits, adapters and sizes you may never use, and you pay for the case or cart around them. Tekton's no-skipped-sizes approach means you can build the same coverage from separate rails, buying each drive size as you need it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="six-point" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 6-Point vs 12-Point in Tekton's Range
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton sells both, but its catalog leans heavily toward 6-point. Almost all of its featured hand and impact sets are 6-point, and 12-point appears as a separate category and in a few large impact kits. That is the right emphasis for most buyers. A 6-point socket grips the flats of a hex fastener with more contact area, and combined with Tekton's radiused corners it is less likely to round off a seized bolt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Twelve-point sockets earn a place in two situations: in tight spaces where you can only swing the ratchet a few degrees and need to re-engage at 30-degree steps, and on 12-point fasteners, which show up on some engine and driveline parts. Our <a href="/6-point-vs-12-point-sockets/">6-point vs 12-point guide</a> goes through those cases. For a first Tekton set, buy 6-point and add a 12-point rail only if a job calls for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A fine-tooth ratchet helps with the same tight-access problem. The 90-tooth ratchet in Tekton's sets needs only a 4-degree swing to engage the next tooth, which reduces the need for 12-point sockets in tight engine bays. Our <a href="/ratchet-tooth-count-explained/">ratchet tooth count guide</a> explains the trade-off between tooth count and tooth strength.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tekton-24335" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Is the Tekton 24335?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plenty of people search for a Tekton 24335 review alongside Tekton sockets, so it is worth clearing up: the 24335 is not a socket set. It is Tekton's 1/2-inch drive torque wrench with a 10 to 150 ft-lb range, which has been a popular budget choice for years. It sits with sockets in searches because it is the tool you use with a 1/2-inch socket to torque lug nuts and suspension bolts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tekton's own page lists the 24335 as a 1/2-inch drive micrometer torque wrench and marks it No Longer Available. Retailers describe the same model as a click-type wrench, which it is: micrometer refers to how the setting is dialled in, and the wrench clicks when the set torque is reached. The user manual gives accuracy of plus or minus 4 percent and says to store the wrench at its lowest setting, 10 ft-lb, to protect that accuracy.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own one, it remains covered by Tekton's warranty for failures. Routine recalibration is a separate paid service, which our Tekton origin and warranty guide prices at $50 if the wrench is in spec and $75 if it needs adjustment. Tekton notes that its micrometer wrenches do not come with a calibration certificate and points buyers who need one toward its split-beam torque wrenches. If you are buying new, choose one of Tekton's current 1/2-inch drive torque wrenches.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="origin-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where Tekton Sockets Are Made, and the Warranty
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton's product pages answer the origin question directly. The sockets in every set we checked are listed as made in Taiwan, and where a set comes on rails and trays, those are listed as made in the USA. That applies to impact sockets too: the 1/2-inch and 3/8-inch impact sets both list sockets made in Taiwan with US-made rails and trays. Each product page states its origin under Specifications, and the socket itself carries a country-of-origin marking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Across its whole lineup, Tekton publishes a breakdown of about 82 percent Taiwan and 12 percent USA, with the rest from Germany, Canada and a few European countries. Tekton says its Michigan facilities make wrenches, picks, hooks and pry bars from US steel; the sockets in the sets we checked are not part of that US share, though their rails are. Taiwan is one of the world's main hand-tool exporters, and as our <a href="/where-are-tekton-tools-made/">origin guide</a> explains, the brand's specification and quality control matter more than the country.
          </p>

          <p>
            The warranty is the strongest part of the deal. Tekton says no time limit, no receipt, and Tekton pays shipping on the replacement, and it covers tools however you came to own them. A cracked socket is replaced through an online form with a photo. The limits are judgment calls rather than published exclusions: a chrome socket split on an impact gun is misuse, and counterfeit tools from marketplace sellers are not covered. Buy from Tekton.com or a known dealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            How do they compare? Our <a href="/tekton-vs-stanley/">Tekton vs Stanley comparison</a> sets them against the most common hardware-store alternative, and our <a href="/tekton-vs-gearwrench/">Tekton vs GearWrench comparison</a> covers the next step up in price. On paper, Tekton's sockets are specified in line with better-known mid-price brands, with a simpler warranty than most, and they trail premium brands mainly on finish refinement and the convenience of a truck at your door.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Chrome Sockets on an Impact Gun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tekton's chrome sockets are for ratchets and breaker bars. On an impact wrench the hard chrome-vanadium can crack, which is dangerous and is treated as misuse. Buy the phosphate-finished impact line for any powered impact tool, even for one quick job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Biggest Kit for Value</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 200-plus piece set looks cheap per piece, but much of the count is bits, adapters and sizes you will never use. Tekton sells the same parts on rails, so buying the drive size you need first and adding later usually costs less.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the 24335 Is a Socket Set</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Tekton 24335 is a 1/2-inch drive 10-150 ft-lb torque wrench, not a set of sockets, and Tekton now lists it as no longer available. If you need one new, choose a current Tekton torque wrench instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Defaulting to 12-Point Sockets</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Twelve-point sockets are useful for tight swing arcs and 12-point fasteners, but they grip a hex bolt less securely. Tekton's catalog is built around 6-point for good reason. Start with 6-point and add a 12-point rail only when a job needs it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying From an Unknown Marketplace Seller</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tekton warns that some marketplace sellers offer used, older or fake tools. Counterfeits carry no Tekton warranty, and older stock may differ from current specs. Buy from Tekton.com or a known dealer if the warranty matters to you.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Tekton sockets any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for home mechanics and most tradespeople. Tekton publishes its socket steel grades, uses stamped markings on chrome sockets and laser-plus-stamped markings on impact sockets, rates proof torque above the ANSI minimum, and backs everything with a no-receipt lifetime warranty. Premium brands still lead on finish refinement and in-person service.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where are Tekton impact sockets made?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In Taiwan. Tekton's product pages for its impact socket sets list the sockets as made in Taiwan and the rails and trays as made in the USA. Every product page states its country of origin under Specifications, and the sockets carry an origin marking, so you can check any individual item before buying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What steel are Tekton sockets made from?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tekton lists chromium-vanadium grades (50BV30 and 6140) for its chrome hand sockets. Its impact sockets mostly use 50BV30 chromium-vanadium, with 4140 chromium-molybdenum for the largest sizes in some sets, finished in manganese phosphate. Heat treatment, wall thickness and finish are what make them suitable for impact use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the Tekton 24335?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a 1/2-inch drive click-type torque wrench with a 10 to 150 ft-lb range. Tekton calls it a micrometer torque wrench, the manual gives plus or minus 4 percent accuracy, and Tekton's site now lists it as no longer available. It is often searched alongside sockets because it pairs with 1/2-inch sockets for lug nuts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Tekton socket set should I buy first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 3/8-inch drive set with shallow and deep sockets in SAE and metric covers most car and household work. Add 1/4-inch drive for small fasteners and 1/2-inch for suspension and lug nuts. If you use an impact wrench, buy a separate impact set rather than using chrome sockets on it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Tekton sockets have a lifetime warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Tekton's warranty has no time limit and needs no receipt, Tekton pays shipping on the replacement, and it covers tools regardless of where you bought them. Claims go through an online form with photos. Counterfeits are not covered, and a chrome socket broken on an impact gun counts as misuse rather than a defect.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Tekton sockets 6-point or 12-point?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are available, but most Tekton sets are 6-point. The 6-point design with radiused corners grips a fastener's flats and is less likely to round off a tight bolt. Twelve-point sockets are sold separately and in a few large impact kits for tight-access work and 12-point fasteners.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

